  Bugs Found

  CRITICAL

  1. resistgate-extension/ is a stale duplicate — The resistgate-extension/ directory contains the same
  files as the root but with an outdated background.js. The uncommitted changes add setUninstallURL to
  both. This directory will cause confusion and potential packaging issues if loaded as the extension.

  2. host_permissions: <all_urls> is over-broad — The manifest requests <all_urls> host permission. This
  is only needed for declarativeNetRequest to redirect blocked sites. However, <all_urls> is the nuclear
  option and will trigger a Chrome Web Store review warning. You should declare only the domains the user
  actually blocks, or use declarativeNetRequestWithHostAccess with more targeted permissions.

  MAJOR

  3. WELCOME_SHOWN_KEY is declared but never read or written (background.js:8) — The key welcomeShown is    defined as a constant but never used anywhere. The welcome page opens on every onInstalled with reason
  === 'install' (which Chrome handles correctly), but there's no mechanism to prevent re-showing on
  updates.

                                                                                                  4. Race condition in initializeExtension — initializationPromise is set to null in the finally block. If
   two callers hit it near-simultaneously after the first resolves, the second call sees initialized ===    true and returns immediately — this is fine. But if a call arrives while initializationPromise is being
  awaited and initialized is still false, the second call correctly awaits the same promise. However, if
  initializationPromise resolves and is set to null before the first await in the try block returns, a
  concurrent call could start a second initialization. The guard if (initializationPromise) prevents this
  in practice, but the finally { initializationPromise = null } pattern creates a brief window where        initialized is true and initializationPromise is null, which is the intended steady state. Low risk in
  practice but the pattern is fragile.

  5. Storage writes have no mutex — updateSettings in the message handler does getSettings → sanitize →     saveSettings. If two messages arrive rapidly (e.g., popup add-site + options save), the second write can   overwrite the first's changes. The updateQueue in queueRulesUpdate only queues rule updates, not
  settings writes. The grantTemporaryAccess function also does read-modify-write on temporaryAccess         without queuing.

                                                                                          6. chrome.runtime.onMessage handler uses void (async () => { ... })() pattern — This works because
  return true keeps the channel open, but if sendResponse is called after the extension context is          invalidated (e.g., service worker restarts), the call silently fails. No retry mechanism exists.
  7. License verification uses atob() and crypto.subtle in service worker — Service workers support
  crypto.subtle in MV3, but the atob usage and string manipulation in base64UrlDecode could fail in edge
  cases with non-ASCII characters. More critically, str2ab uses str.charCodeAt(i) which mangles characters   > 255. For base64url this is fine since the charset is ASCII, but it's a latent assumption.

              MINOR
  8. console.log statements left in production — background.js lines 351, 400, 1089, 1099, 1110 all have
  console.log calls that will appear in the service worker console. Not a bug per se, but noisy for         production.

  9. Duplicate normalizeDomain() function — The exact same function exists in background.js,
  popup/popup.js, and friction-page/script.js. Any bug fix or behavior change must be applied in all three   places.
  10. Friction page startTime is set on "I'm Ready" click (script.js:57) — If the user clicks "Start        Challenge", then clicks "Cancel" and restarts, startTime is reset on the confirm click, but if they
  reload the page, startTime is null and Date.now() - null gives NaN. The completeChallenge function uses
  Date.now() - startTime which would produce NaN, breaking earn-access time checks.

  11. Friction page XSS risk in innerHTML assignment (script.js:160) — proPrecheck.innerHTML =              sections.join('') injects HTML that includes friction.customChallengePrompt via escapeHtml(). This is
  safe because escapeHtml is used, but the pattern is fragile. One missed call and it's XSS.

  12. Options page pro-precheck area uses innerHTML with escapeHtml — Same concern as above. Currently      safe, but brittle pattern.

  13. temporaryAccess stored as object keyed by domain — If a user blocks youtube.com and www.youtube.com   (both normalize to different values depending on input), they get separate access entries. But
  buildBlockingRules deduplicates by normalizeDomain, so both domains get blocked. The access lookup uses   the exact domain from the blocklist entry's urlPattern, which should match, but the
  isDomainTemporarilyAccessible check in the background uses the domain key directly, not a normalized
  lookup.
  14. Analytics only track Pro users — recordBlockedVisit and recordAnalyticsEvent silently return if
  !hasProAccess(settings). This means free users get zero analytics data, so upgrading to Pro starts with
  an empty dashboard. This is by design per ROADMAP.md but worth noting as a UX gap.
  15. isProUser() in options.js and hasProAccess() in background.js use slightly different logic —
  options.js:949-958 checks cachedSettings.subscription.tier directly, while background.js:933-946 uses     sanitizeSettings which normalizes. Both treat no-expiresAt as valid Pro, but the options page relies on
  potentially stale cachedSettings rather than re-reading from storage.
  ---                                                                                                       Missing / Incomplete

  1. No content_scripts declared — The extension relies entirely on declarativeNetRequest for blocking.     This means it cannot inject any UI into blocked pages. The friction page is a full redirect, not an
  overlay. This is fine but means no inline dismissal/countdown UI on the original page.

  2. welcomeShown key is never written — WELCOME_SHOWN_KEY is defined but never used. The welcome page      re-shows only on reason === 'install' which Chrome handles, but there's no opt-out mechanism if a user
  closes it and wants to revisit.
  3. No content security policy — The manifest has no content_security_policy. The friction page and
  options page load Google Fonts via external <link>. This works in MV3 (CSP for extension pages is
  relaxed by default), but is worth noting.

  4. No internationalization (i18n) — All UI text is hardcoded English.
  5. friction-page is not declared in web_accessible_resources — But it's loaded via
  chrome.runtime.getURL() in a redirect, which works without being in web_accessible_resources. However,
  the welcome/welcome.html IS declared in web_accessible_resources with <all_urls>, which is overly broad   — it only needs to be accessible to the extension itself.

  6. No error boundary in friction page — If chrome.runtime.sendMessage fails (extension context
  invalidated, service worker asleep), the user is stuck on the friction page with no way to proceed.
  7. No declarativeNetRequest rule limit handling — Chrome limits session rules to 5000 (or 300 on some
  versions). No guard exists against adding too many blocklist entries.

  8. Paywall modal has a "Go to Pricing" button that calls openPricingPage() which sends a message to the
  background. This works, but the email validation is basic (/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/) and the pricing   URL leaks extensionId and deviceId in query params.

                                                      9. No graceful degradation for offline scenarios — refreshEntitlement makes a fetch() call that will
  fail offline. The extension continues working (local storage), but the user might see confusing error
  states.
